mode systems

# autodiff.tw: how the tape and the backward pass interact with a device.
#
# src/tensor.tw records a flat tape: each TapeEntry holds an op, the indices of
# earlier entries, and the output tensor, and `backward` walks it in reverse
# accumulating cotangents. The question this file answers is what changes when
# `out` may live on a GPU, and the short answer is less than it looks.
#
# The tape never inspects the bytes of `out`. It stores it and hands it to a vjp
# function. Only the vjp functions look inside, and the vjp functions are built
# from the same forward kernels: vjp_matmul is a transpose2d and two mm calls,
# vjp_softmax is a dot along a run and two elementwise ops, vjp_binary is
# elementwise plus a sum_to_shape. So the backward pass introduces no primitive
# the forward pass does not already have, and if the forward kernels are
# device-resident then the backward ones are too, by construction. There is no
# separate backward device path to write, and that is worth stating because it
# is the part of this design most likely to be assumed hard.
#
# Two places would force a transfer per node if written naively, and both are
# avoidable. They are the whole content of this file.

import "bytes.tw" as bytes
import "src/gpu/device" as dev
import "src/gpu/buffer" as buf
import "src/gpu/dispatch" as disp
import "tensor.tw" as tensor

# ---- 1. accumulate ---------------------------------------------------------
#
# `accumulate` adds an incoming cotangent into a node's slot. On the host it is
# a loop over a buffer. Written naively on a device it would be: download the
# slot, add on the host, upload the slot. That is two crossings per tape node,
# which for a hundred-node graph is two hundred crossings at roughly 80us each
# per training step, and it would make a device backward pass slower than the
# host one by a wide margin regardless of how fast the kernels were.
#
# So it is the elementwise add kernel writing into the existing device buffer.
# This is the only place in the backend where a buffer is written twice, and the
# only place that departs from the value semantics the rest of the design leans
# on, so it needs the safety argument spelled out rather than assumed:
#
#   - A cotangent slot is private to one backward pass. Nothing outside
#     `backward` holds a reference to it.
#   - The tape is walked in reverse index order on the host, one node at a time,
#     with a finish after each launch. Two accumulations into the same slot are
#     therefore separated by a synchronise and cannot race.
#   - The slot is never a tensor the user can observe. `backward` returns fresh
#     tensors built from the finished slots.
#
# Take any of those three away and this becomes unsafe. In particular, the day
# the queue is allowed to run ahead (docs/gpu.md section 3 says that is the
# first optimisation to take), the second point stops holding and this needs an
# explicit dependency between the two launches.
fn accumulate(c: disp.Ctx, slot: buf.DeviceTensor,
              incoming: buf.DeviceTensor) -> Res[Unit, Str] {
  let inc: buf.DeviceTensor = buf.ensure_device(c.pool, incoming)?
  if slot.buf.length != inc.buf.length {
    return Err("gpu.accumulate: cotangent and slot differ in length")
  }
  let k: dev.Kernel = dev.kernel(c.program, "k_accumulate")?
  dev.arg_buffer(k, 0, slot.buf)?
  dev.arg_buffer(k, 1, inc.buf)?
  dev.arg_i64(k, 2, slot.buf.length)?
  let g: Arr[I64] = arr_new()
  push(g, slot.buf.length)
  disp.run(c, k, g, arr_new())
}

# ---- 2. touched ------------------------------------------------------------
#
# `touched` is the flag array saying whether a cotangent slot has been written
# yet, so that the first contribution can be a move rather than an add into
# zeros. It is host-side bookkeeping over node indices and never touches tensor
# data, so it stays on the host and costs nothing.
#
# This is recorded rather than left implicit because it is the kind of array
# that gets swept onto the device by a well-meaning refactor that notices it is
# indexed in the same loop as the tensors. It is Arr[Bool] over the tape, not
# over elements, and moving it would be pure loss.
struct BackwardState {
  # One slot per tape entry. LocHost with an empty host buffer means untouched.
  cot: Arr[buf.DeviceTensor],
  touched: Arr[Bool],
}

fn new_state(n: I64) -> BackwardState {
  let cot: Arr[buf.DeviceTensor] = arr_new()
  let touched: Arr[Bool] = arr_new()
  let i: I64 = 0
  while i < n {
    push(cot, buf.from_host(arr_new(), arr_new()))
    push(touched, false)
    i = i + 1
  }
  BackwardState { cot: cot, touched: touched }
}

# The first contribution to a slot takes the incoming tensor whole, which saves
# both an allocation and a zero-fill. Every later one accumulates in place.
# src/tensor.tw's accumulate makes the same distinction for the same reason.
fn contribute(c: disp.Ctx, st: BackwardState, node: I64,
              g: buf.DeviceTensor) -> Res[Unit, Str] {
  if !st.touched[node] {
    st.cot[node] = g
    st.touched[node] = true
    return Ok(unit)
  }
  accumulate(c, st.cot[node], g)
}

# ---- device memory is the resource that runs out ---------------------------
#
# A tape entry pins its output alive. On the host that costs memory. On a device
# it costs device memory, which on the card in the development machine is 8GB
# shared with the display, and a training step whose activations are all
# resident can exceed that where the host copy would have been fine.
#
# The eviction rule in src/gpu/buffer.tw covers it, because an activation whose
# host copy exists is LocBoth and therefore evictable. But the honest statement
# is that this is the resource this design is most likely to exhaust first, and
# the mitigation is a fallback rather than a fix: the run gets slower, not
# wrong.
#
# There is a known flaw in the eviction order for exactly this case. Oldest
# first evicts weights before activations, and weights are the tensors that
# should never be evicted, because they are re-uploaded every step forever while
# an activation is used once. Pinning tape leaves is the fix and it is not
# written, because pinning without a measurement of how often it bites would be
# tuning against an imagined profile.
fn should_pin(tp: tensor.Tape, node: I64) -> Bool {
  let i: I64 = 0
  while i < len(tp.leaves) {
    if tp.leaves[i] == node { return true }
    i = i + 1
  }
  false
}

# ---- ops with no device kernel ---------------------------------------------
#
# sort, argsort, topk, median and einsum have no kernel. Their operands come
# down, they run on the host, and their result stays on the host. That is
# correct and it is slow, and the dispatch model already accounts for it,
# because the next device op will see a cold operand and charge itself for the
# re-upload.
#
# A backward pass through a median is going to be slow. That is a true statement
# about the op and not a flaw in the design, and it is written down so that the
# first person to profile it does not go looking for a bug.
fn has_device_kernel(op: tensor.Op) -> Bool {
  match op {
    OpAdd => true, OpSub => true, OpMul => true, OpDiv => true, OpMod => true,
    OpMaximum => true, OpMinimum => true,
    OpGreater => true, OpLess => true, OpGreaterEqual => true,
    OpLessEqual => true, OpEqual => true,
    OpNeg => true, OpRelu => true, OpSquare => true, OpClip => true,
    OpWhere => true, OpMatMul => true,
    OpSum => true, OpMean => true, OpMax => true, OpMin => true, OpProd => true,
    OpArgmax => true, OpArgmin => true,
    OpConv2D => true, OpMaxPool2D => true,
    OpTranspose => true,
    # Softmax and logsumexp have kernels and are not listed, because they call
    # exp and src/gpu/softmax.tw refuses them until NEEDS-68's algorithm exists
    # in kernel source. Listing them here would be the one edit that turns a
    # correct backend into one that is off by an ulp on every sigmoid.
    _ => false,
  }
}

# ---- second order ----------------------------------------------------------
#
# `hessian` in src/tensor.tw runs forward-mode jets over the same kernels. A jet
# doubles the arithmetic and keeps the shapes, so nothing about residency
# changes and every kernel here would work over a jet's value and derivative
# parts as two buffers.
#
# No device work is planned for jets in the first version and a jet run stays on
# the host entirely. That is a decision and not an omission: doubling the
# kernel count to serve an op that appears in one place would be a lot of
# surface for no measured demand, and the pair-of-buffers representation should
# be designed once rather than twice.
fn jets_on_device() -> Bool = false
